\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The Wilson symbol in the last plane}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take #{n=2m\ge 4}, #{k=n-2}, and one minimal spin copy with canonical volume grading, as in \ref{fdip-01A1}. Use independent scalar fields in the ordered planes #{(1,2),\ldots,(n-1,n)} and give the last plane constant curvature. Let #{H'_\lambda=C'(aD'_W-\lambda\bbI)} on the inner spin-site space #{W}, with #{C'} the ordered inner volume grading. At #{\mu=aM} the dimensionless Hermitian Wilson operator is unitarily equivalent to the \vocabk{ordered evaluation}{fdip-01C3} for the two forward shifts of the last plane, with
##{d(y)=-\sin y,\qquad F(x,y)=H'_{\mu -2+\cos y+\cos x}-\ui\sin x\,\bbI .}}

\proof{\p{Choose Clifford coordinates with inner generators #{\varepsilon'_j\otimes\bbI}, last generators #{C'\otimes\sigma_3,C'\otimes\sigma_2} and full grading #{C'\otimes\sigma_1}. These satisfy all the Clifford relations and the volume sign by multiplication, and form a minimal copy by \ref{fdip-01A1}. In the #{\sigma_3} basis the inner graded kernel enters with #{\sigma_1}. The outer Wilson terms add #{2-\cos x-\cos y} to its scalar mass term. The centered products #{\ui\sigma_1\sigma_3=\sigma_2} and #{\ui\sigma_1\sigma_2=-\sigma_3} give the displayed imaginary upper entry and real diagonal. All inner operators commute with the outer shifts. The coefficients of positive and negative powers of the first shift are initially independent of the second angle, so their ordered evaluation gives the exact operator.}}
